  "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x+5)-1,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2y+5)-1\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2y+5)-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2y+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2y+5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(2y+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5) \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
